  "body": "Six fresh, paired width-eight fits test fixed spatial-input gains of 1 and 53 on the same Monte Carlo return labels. Inverse first-layer scaling matches initial functions before optimization. All six runs use 80 epochs and fixed final checkpoints.\n\nThe scalar screen fails: **3/6 conditions**. All three training-excess conditions pass; all three required validation improvements fail. Mean training error above the empirical input-alias floor falls **42.11%**, while mean validation MSE rises **9.73%**. Validation improves slightly in two seeds and worsens in one. This is ordinary input conditioning, with no autonomous or novel-architecture claim.\n\nThe independent audit agrees on all 300 saved-model readouts / 73,842 rows and all six conditions. The six fit intervals total 47.86 seconds; the enclosing supervised worker takes 62.56 seconds. Independent audit takes another 15.80 seconds. These are one-run CPU timings, not a latency benchmark.\n\nThe archive includes all initial/final weights, predictions, update/order journals, qualification, source, audit and reporting evidence. Every archive member was checked against its SHA-256 manifest. Historical input caches remain separate dependencies from the original scalar and capacity releases.\n\nBoth gains remain in the proposed fresh autonomous comparison regardless of this failed scalar screen. That next comparison is unexecuted and requires its own qualification and frozen plan.\n\n[Results and all seeds](https://github.com/kw2828/OpenJev/blob/main/research/otto-conditioning-results.md) \u00b7 [Protocol](https://github.com/kw2828/OpenJev/blob/main/research/otto-conditioning-protocol.md) \u00b7 [Original scalar dependency](https://github.com/kw2828/OpenJev/releases/tag/otto-return-value-v1) \u00b7 [Capacity dependency](https://github.com/kw2828/OpenJev/releases/tag/otto-capacity-v1)\n",
